<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hill Forts of Rajasthan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">Hill Forts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> are a group of six forts inscribed together as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2013: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbhalgarh">Kumbhalgarh</a>, Ranthambore, Gagron, Amber and Jaisalmer. Built and enlarged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states between the eighth and eighteenth centuries, they use hills, rivers and forests as natural defences and enclose palaces, temples, water <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and towns, illustrating Rajput military architecture and court culture.</p></div>
